# Patent Claim Draft

## Claim 1

A method for determining a position of a railway vehicle traveling along a railway track, the method comprising:

receiving, at a Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) receiver disposed onboard the railway vehicle, GNSS signals from a plurality of satellites, each GNSS signal comprising a carrier phase measurement and a pseudorange measurement affected by an ionospheric propagation delay;

acquiring, from an odometer disposed onboard the railway vehicle, odometry data representative of a traveled distance and a velocity of the railway vehicle along the track;

retrieving, from a stored digital track map, track geometry data corresponding to a current track section, the track geometry data comprising at least curvature, elevation profile, and track coordinates constraining the railway vehicle to a one-dimensional path of travel;

computing a constrained position estimate of the railway vehicle along the one-dimensional path by fusing the odometry data with the track geometry data, thereby generating a track-constrained trajectory hypothesis independent of the GNSS carrier phase measurements;

projecting, for each of the plurality of satellites, an expected geometric range and an expected line-of-sight direction between the railway vehicle and the satellite, based on the constrained position estimate and known satellite ephemeris data;

estimating an ionospheric bias term for each carrier phase measurement by comparing the carrier phase measurement, corrected for the expected geometric range derived from the constrained position estimate, against a predicted ionosphere-free combination derived from a dual-frequency measurement or a reference ionospheric model, so as to isolate a residual attributable to ionospheric propagation delay;

filtering the estimated ionospheric bias term over time using a recursive estimator, wherein the track-constrained trajectory hypothesis serves as a deterministic motion constraint reducing an observability requirement for separating the ionospheric bias term from a receiver clock bias term and a carrier phase ambiguity term;

correcting the carrier phase measurement for each satellite using the filtered ionospheric bias term to obtain a corrected carrier phase measurement; and

computing a final position of the railway vehicle by combining the corrected carrier phase measurements from the plurality of satellites with the constrained position estimate derived from the odometry data and the track geometry data, using a fusion filter.

---

## Claim 2 (dependent)

The method of claim 1, wherein the recursive estimator is a Kalman filter or an extended Kalman filter having a state vector comprising the ionospheric bias term for each satellite, the receiver clock bias term, a receiver clock drift term, and a track-curvilinear position and velocity of the railway vehicle.

## Claim 3 (dependent)

The method of claim 1, wherein estimating the ionospheric bias term comprises computing a difference between a first pseudorange derived from a first GNSS frequency and a second pseudorange derived from a second GNSS frequency, and apportioning the difference between the ionospheric bias term and a multipath term using the track-constrained trajectory hypothesis as a bound on a maximum admissible position error.

## Claim 4 (dependent)

The method of claim 1, further comprising updating the digital track map based on a discrepancy between the constrained position estimate and the final position, and storing the updated digital track map for use in a subsequent iteration of the method.

## Claim 5 (dependent)

The method of claim 1, wherein the fusion filter weights the corrected carrier phase measurements according to a satellite elevation angle computed from the constrained position estimate and the known satellite ephemeris data, such that carrier phase measurements associated with lower elevation angles and correspondingly higher estimated ionospheric bias uncertainty are assigned a lower weight.

## Claim 6 (dependent)

The method of claim 1, wherein the track geometry data further comprises a track cant and a track gauge, and wherein the constrained position estimate further accounts for a lateral offset of the GNSS receiver relative to a rail centerline resulting from the track cant.

## Claim 7 (independent, system claim)

An onboard railway positioning system comprising:
- a GNSS receiver configured to receive carrier phase and pseudorange measurements from a plurality of satellites;
- an odometer configured to output odometry data;
- a memory storing a digital track map comprising track geometry data;
- a processor coupled to the GNSS receiver, the odometer, and the memory, configured to execute the method of claim 1.

---

